{
  "format": "sfxgen/1",
  "name": "Door latch click (hollow-core)",
  "description": "A cheap, light hollow-core bedroom door flicked shut on a carpeted landing: more pock than thud. First the bolt of the small tubular latch knocks back off the strike plate, a light, higher tack. Then it springs into the keeper, the lower and louder half of the ka-chunk, a dead rattly clack with pressed steel and plastic in it. At almost the same instant the thin door skins slap the stop and the cardboard-honeycomb cavity between them answers: a boxy, hollow pock, gone almost at once, with very little weight under it. Then the loose skin and the handle buzz against the frame for a moment. The room is small and soft: carpet, a short dull answer.",
